\description{
Continuous scales for symbols and patterns that are perceived as areas or volumes, so that the
area or volume, rather than the diameter, is proportional to the data value.
\code{tm_scale_continuous_area()} is used for symbols that are perceived as areas, such as the
\code{size} of circles and squares in \code{\link[=tm_symbols]{tm_symbols()}}, and for the \code{pattern_density} of dot
patterns (see \code{\link[=tm_patterns]{tm_patterns()}}), for which the number of dots per area is proportional to
the data value. \code{tm_scale_continuous_volume()} is used for symbols that are perceived as volumes,
such as spheres and cubes.
}
\details{
A visual value grows in a number of dimensions \code{d}: a line width and a
transparency in one dimension, the area of a symbol and the number of dots per area of a dot
pattern in two dimensions, and the volume of a sphere in three dimensions. To make the
amount that grows proportional to the data, the visual value (e.g. the diameter of a symbol)
is proportional to the data value to the power \code{1/d}. The limits start at 0, so a data value
of 0 corresponds to a visual value of 0, and twice the data value to twice the area or
volume.\tabular{llll}{
   Scale \tab Dimensions \tab Power \tab Typical use \cr
   \code{\link[=tm_scale_continuous]{tm_scale_continuous()}} \tab 1 \tab 1 \tab \code{lwd}, transparency, density of line patterns \cr
   \code{tm_scale_continuous_area()} \tab 2 \tab 1/2 \tab symbol \code{size}, density of dot patterns \cr
   \code{tm_scale_continuous_volume()} \tab 3 \tab 1/3 \tab symbols that look like spheres or cubes \cr
}


Note that the default scale for \code{size} (\code{\link[=tm_scale_continuous]{tm_scale_continuous()}}) already maps to the area, since
the default \code{values} of \code{size} are \code{tm_seq(0, 1, power = "sqrt")}.

These scales should not be confused with \code{\link[=tm_scale_continuous_sqrt]{tm_scale_continuous_sqrt()}}: that function
