\section{Perceptual scaling}{

Areas and volumes are not perceived proportionally: larger symbols are perceived as
smaller than they are. In psychophysics, this is described by a power law: the perceived
magnitude is proportional to the actual magnitude to the power \code{e}, the perception
exponent, with \code{e < 1}. This can be compensated for by using the power \code{1 / (d * e)}
instead of \code{1 / d}. With \code{perceptual = TRUE}, the following perception exponents are used:
\itemize{
\item area: \code{e = 0.875}, which gives the power 0.5716. This is the compensation that was derived
by Flannery (1971) in experiments with graduated circles, and which is also available via
\code{tm_seq(power = "sqrt_perceptual")}.
\item volume: \code{e = 0.75}, which gives the power \code{(1/3) / 0.75 = 0.444}. This value is tentative:
it is not established by empirical studies on map symbols. It is derived as follows. Ekman
and Junge (1961) found that the perceived volumes of real objects are proportional
to their volumes (\code{e} close to 1), but that for perspective drawings of cubes and spheres
\code{e} is about 0.75. They concluded that the volume of a drawing is largely judged by its
area. The power 0.444 follows from this exponent in the same way as
Flannery's power follows from the exponent for areas.
}

A number can also be specified for \code{perceptual}, which is then used as the perception
exponent \code{e} (between 0 and 1, where 1 means no compensation). Note that this is not the
power itself: e.g. \code{perceptual = 0.8} for areas gives the power \code{0.5 / 0.8 = 0.625}. To
specify the power directly, use \code{\link[=tm_scale_continuous]{tm_scale_continuous()}} with
\verb{values = tm_seq(0, 1, power = <power>)}.
}

\examples{
# the area of the bubbles is proportional to the population (as with the default scale)
tm_shape(World) +
	tm_polygons() +
	tm_bubbles(size = "pop_est",
		size.scale = tm_scale_continuous_area())

# compensated for the underestimation of larger areas (Flannery, 1971)
tm_shape(World) +
	tm_polygons() +
	tm_bubbles(size = "pop_est",
		size.scale = tm_scale_continuous_area(perceptual = TRUE))

# bubbles that look like spheres: the volume is proportional to the population
tm_shape(World) +
	tm_polygons() +
	tm_bubbles(size = "pop_est",
		size.scale = tm_scale_continuous_volume(values = c(0, 2)))

# dot density: the number of dots is proportional to the population density
utrecht = NLD_dist[NLD_dist$province == "Utrecht", ]
utrecht$pop_dens = utrecht$population / utrecht$area # units: 1/km^2
tm_shape(utrecht) +
	tm_polygons(fill = "grey95", col = "grey60", lwd = 0.5,
		pattern = "stipple",
		pattern_size = 1.2,
		pattern_density = "pop_dens",
		pattern_density.scale = tm_scale_continuous_area(limits = c(0, 10000)),
		pattern_density.legend = tm_legend("Inhabitants per km2",
			dot.value = "1 dot \u2248 {n} inhabitants"))
}
